WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:07.299
Food is an enormous part of our lives; it
affects our moods, changes our bodies, is

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:12.320
a key part of our culture and celebrations
and, for many of us, is a large part of what

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.190
we think about during the day. In fact, you
could go as far as to say, the whole idea

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:23.599
of civilisation is completely down to food
since it was the collaboration we learnt through

00:00:23.599 --> 00:00:27.880
hunting and then the communities we built
through agriculture that created villages

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:32.590
and towns, as well as technology.
But if you take that sandwich out your mouth

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:38.390
for a second, do you ever wonder how it ended
up on your plate? Where did they grow the

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:43.310
wheat to make the bread? What pig did the
ham come from? Why did they put pickles in

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.570
it? They know you hate pickles. All very important
questions.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:52.830
So today, we are going to have a quick look
at the major problems facing food, glorious

00:00:52.830 --> 00:01:01.170
food.
Most of the world is omnivorous; we eat meat

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:07.700
and plants. Although vegetarianism and veganism
are on the rise, the numbers are still relatively

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:13.990
low. India is way ahead with 40% of the country
being vegetarian but in almost every other

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:18.759
nation, it’s well under 10%.
Animal farming, or husbandry, as it’s called,

00:01:18.759 --> 00:01:26.259
began in the Neolithic era, some 12,000 years
ago. We began to selectively breed the traits

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:32.780
we wanted into cattle and other livestock,
making them fatter and more docile, because

00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:37.460
let’s be honest; you’d rather deal with
this than every morning.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:43.109
As society grew, meat was more commonly the
food of the upper classes, since you needed

00:01:43.109 --> 00:01:48.039
to invest in the care of the animal first,
in order to eat it, especially if you lived

00:01:48.039 --> 00:01:53.079
in a city so far from any hunting grounds.
You can even see this reflected in the English

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:57.329
language. The words for meats generally come
from French;

00:01:57.329 --> 00:02:01.600
beef – boeuf,
mutton – mouton

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:07.209
The English royalty normally spoke in French
and they were the ones eating the animals.

00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:13.170
But the lower classes, those of Germanic origin,
farmed the animals so the actual creature

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:18.680
names normally come from German and Dutch:
Cow – Kuh

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:23.409
Sheep – Schaap
And this leads us to our current problem.

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:29.890
As countries grow richer, you can see their
meat consumption increasing, since it is seen

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:34.799
as a part of a wealthier lifestyle. China
is a good example of this. According to the

00:02:34.799 --> 00:02:38.730
OECD (Organisation for Economic Co-operation
and Development), by 2024, China will be eating

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:45.730
over 10% more beef, almost 25% more poultry
and 5% more pork.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:52.250
But they have a long way to go to catch up
with the carnivorous competition. The USA

00:02:52.250 --> 00:03:00.209
is the world’s biggest meat lover, chowing
down on over 120kg per person every year – that’s

00:03:00.209 --> 00:03:04.769
the weight of 2 average adults. Just to be
clear, I’m not saying that Americans eat

00:03:04.769 --> 00:03:10.879
people, with or without fava beans.
Australia and New Zealand are the only other

00:03:10.879 --> 00:03:16.019
countries who manage over 100kg annually.
Australia because of their barbeque culture

00:03:16.019 --> 00:03:20.530
and New Zealand because what else were they
supposed to do with all those Orcs after the

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:24.610
battle of Mordor?
Producing all this meat uses up an enormous

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:29.750
amount of resources and is one of the biggest
contributors to climate change. The main problem

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:36.049
is that it leads to our never-ending deforestation,
especially in South and Central America. Since

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:42.920
the start of this video, another 2 square
kilometres of trees have been felled down.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.579
They use the land as space for the animals,
but also to grow feed for them, such as corn

00:03:47.579 --> 00:03:53.370
and soya. And less trees, means less CO2 being
absorbed from the atmosphere. It also ruins

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:57.709
a good game of hide and seek.
The second issue is that these animals produce

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:04.319
a lot of gas, especially cows. Methane is
a powerful greenhouse gas and our bovine friends

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:12.760
produce 120kg of it a year, that’s a thousand
times more than a human. So cows are terrible

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.920
roommates, especially with their appalling
taste in mooosic.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:21.180
The way we keep huge herds of animals together
also means the risk of diseases spreading

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:26.669
is far higher than it used to be, and much
faster. Outbreaks of things like avian flu

00:04:26.669 --> 00:04:32.800
resulted in the mass culling of millions of
poultry, in an effort to limit it. Many animals

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:37.340
are given antibiotics but regulations are
changing now, since they become less effective

00:04:37.340 --> 00:04:42.090
over time and can lead to the evolution of
a Superbug.

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:46.180
What do you think was the first animal we
started breeding?

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:52.660
Of course, it was the dog, and we began doing
it over 30,000 years ago. So, that is how

00:04:52.660 --> 00:04:58.120
long we have been genetically modifying life
around us. The huge varieties of breeds of

00:04:58.120 --> 00:05:03.110
dog are all descended from wolves but we have
been using artificial selection, as opposed

00:05:03.110 --> 00:05:07.509
to natural selection, for tens of thousands
of years.

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:12.400
A scientist called Dmitry Belyaev tried to
look at how the process works by doing the

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:19.539
same with foxes in Siberia. From 1959 they
started selecting the friendliest foxes and

00:05:19.539 --> 00:05:24.750
killing the rest so that their tameness quality
would become a part of the breed. The experiment

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:30.240
went on for some 40 years, they didn’t have
anything better to do in Siberia, but the

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.169
results were pretty impressive. The foxes
could be walked and act as pets. The most

00:05:35.169 --> 00:05:39.960
interesting part was that they displayed similar
changes to domestic dogs, with their floppy

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:45.490
ears and differently patterned fur. Look at
a wolf’s ears and then at a Dachshund, they

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:50.780
are so different, but we weren’t breeding
dogs to have floppy ears, what would be the

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:55.919
point? It turns out that a friendlier, tamer
animal is one that has a lower epinephrine

00:05:55.919 --> 00:06:02.539
level, also called adrenaline. So different
hormone levels led to different physical development.

00:06:02.539 --> 00:06:07.400
It also affected melanin production, which
is what gives the fur its colour, so that’s

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:12.280
why they saw changes there as well.
Our artificial selection was not limited to

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:18.879
animals of course. Almost every crop, fruit
and vegetable in the supermarket today has

00:06:18.879 --> 00:06:25.129
been altered from its original form by farmers
selecting the traits they want and mixing

00:06:25.129 --> 00:06:30.150
plants where they can. The lemon is a good
example of this, being a hybrid of the citron

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:34.849
and the bitter orange. Without mankind’s
input, it’s unlikely that the lemon would

00:06:34.849 --> 00:06:39.229
have existed, and no gin and tonic would ever
have been good enough.

00:06:39.229 --> 00:06:44.960
Then the big jump came with the discovery
of DNA. Friedrich Miescher first isolated

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:50.590
it in 1869 and then Watson and Crick gave
us the famous double helix structure that

00:06:50.590 --> 00:06:58.479
we know today, in 1953. By 1982 we had the
first patented GM organism; an oil eating

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:03.930
microorganism owned by the Exxon Oil Company,
designed to eat up oil spills.

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:10.780
So how do you genetically engineer an organism?
How do you splice a tomato with a fish for

00:07:10.780 --> 00:07:15.379
example? This was a real thing, by the way.
They were using the anti-freeze gene of the

00:07:15.379 --> 00:07:20.039
White Flounder fish to help tomatoes survive
better in the frost. Apparently, hats and

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:24.849
scarves were not an option.
Here’s a very basic overview of how genetic

00:07:24.849 --> 00:07:30.509
engineering works. DNA is a group of molecules
in the form of a double helix and they combine

00:07:30.509 --> 00:07:38.039
in pairs to make strands, called chromosomes.
For humans, there are 46 chromosomes and together,

00:07:38.039 --> 00:07:43.750
these make up our genome, so it’s this collection
of information that makes a human a human,

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:49.009
a dog a dog, and a cat a mean, ungrateful
psychopath with a cute face.

00:07:49.009 --> 00:07:54.879
You search though genomes for the traits you
want; bigger leaves, less water usage, fishy

00:07:54.879 --> 00:08:00.319
freeze proofing, whatever you’re after.
Then you isolate the bit of the genome that

00:08:00.319 --> 00:08:06.080
manages that trait and you cut it out, using
enzymes – it’s just easier than scissors

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:11.409
apparently. You then stick that bit of genetic
material into a thing called a plasmid – which

00:08:11.409 --> 00:08:16.280
is sort of like an extra storage space you
find in some bacteria. Now you need to put

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:21.800
it into the cells you want to modify. You
can literally fire it in, with a thing called

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:28.169
a gene gun, using tiny metal particles as
bullets. You can also use bacteria that will

00:08:28.169 --> 00:08:33.560
infect the cells you want to change, and your
genetic code can just hop out, like a Trojan

00:08:33.560 --> 00:08:36.909
jumping out of one of those wooden horses
they love so much.

00:08:36.909 --> 00:08:42.579
Our main use for GMOs is as crops and we use
the process to make plants more resistant

00:08:42.579 --> 00:08:48.240
to the problems of their environment, potentially
giving farmers a far bigger harvest. In the

00:08:48.240 --> 00:08:55.360
US alone, it’s reported to have increased
agricultural production by almost $100 billion.

00:08:55.360 --> 00:09:00.920
The ability to alter crops quickly if the
environment changes will also be a big help,

00:09:00.920 --> 00:09:06.430
especially if we want to move away from meat
consumption. So what is the big controversy

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:15.250
around GMOs? Well, so far, there is no scientific
evidence that they are in any way unsafe or

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:21.440
detrimental to human health. But many people
wonder if it is a good idea to be poking around

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:26.380
with life in this way. It’s not just the
GMOs that have changed, since they affect

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:31.760
the environment around them too, such as the
animals that need the GMO crops to survive.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.019
As we learnt in the documentary Jurassic Park,
when you mess about with life, things can

00:09:36.019 --> 00:09:37.510
go wrong pretty quickly. Oh, that wasn’t
a documentary.

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.149
It’s probably GMOs association with our
next problem that is part of their negative

00:09:39.149 --> 00:09:42.910
public image.
From mildew on our lettuce, to flies in our

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:48.550
soup and ants in our pants, it sometimes feels
like we’re in an endless war to keep pests

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:53.810
off our stuff. They are the annoying little
brothers of the animal world. And this is

00:09:53.810 --> 00:09:58.130
why we turn to our weapon of bug destruction;
the pesticide.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:04.600
For farmers, there are a number of ways you
can lose yield; animals, insects, disease

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:08.850
and weeds, as well as extreme weather events
such as drought and flooding.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:13.860
The yield is the amount of usable product
you end up with, so that would be the edible

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:20.310
grain for corn, the white fibre for cotton.
If we let everything grow completely unassisted,

00:10:20.310 --> 00:10:26.990
it’s estimated we could lose 80% of all
cotton, 50% of wheat and over 30% of most

00:10:26.990 --> 00:10:33.700
other key crops. There are a number of ways
we try to combat this, such as mechanically

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:39.190
tilling the ground, using software to plant
in the best places at the best times, and

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:45.480
lots of others. But it is still weeds and
insects that are the biggest issue, so we

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:51.980
turn to herbicides and pesticides.
The Sumerians are the earliest users of pesticides,

00:10:51.980 --> 00:10:59.829
using a sulphur compound to control insects
and mites, about 4500 years ago. But as technology

00:10:59.829 --> 00:11:04.930
developed, and our understanding of plants
grew, we were able to create better targeted

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:11.230
protections. Today, one of the most widely
used is the notorious herbicide Roundup, made

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:15.480
by Monsanto.
Roundup is mainly glyphosate, and it works

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:23.300
by inhibiting an enzyme, called EPSP synthase,
that plants need to survive. But if it kills

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:29.670
all plants then isn’t that farming genocide?
This is where the GMOs come in. Companies

00:11:29.670 --> 00:11:37.230
have designed crops that have an enzyme that
does what EPSP does, but isn’t affected

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:44.980
by Roundup. Okay, so if we’re spraying this
on our food; is it safe? Um, yes and no. You

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:49.579
certainly wouldn’t want to do shots of it
and it will irritate your skin if you touch

00:11:49.579 --> 00:11:55.360
it. But, given the huge quantities that are
used globally, it’s safe to say that it’s

00:11:55.360 --> 00:12:01.820
not dangerously toxic since most of us are
ingesting traces of it pretty regularly. The

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:07.519
World Health Organisation have said that glyphosate
is probably a carcinogen, it causes cancer,

00:12:07.519 --> 00:12:13.589
but the amounts required would be much much
higher than would ever be in the food people

00:12:13.589 --> 00:12:17.970
are eating.
The big issues are that, since glyphosate

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:24.589
kills most plants, it can easily get into
the water and damage the surrounding environment,

00:12:24.589 --> 00:12:29.630
including aquatic flora. There have also been
new weeds appearing that are now resistant

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:35.440
to the herbicide and it’s been suggested
that the over use of glyphosate has accelerated

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:39.949
this process, because evolution is all about
repetition.

00:12:39.949 --> 00:12:44.930
So if you want to avoid pesticides, you just
go organic, right?

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:50.209
I’m afraid not. This is one of the biggest
misunderstandings in the supermarket, up there

00:12:50.209 --> 00:12:55.089
with; why haven’t they opened a third lane,
why is that woman buying just cat food and

00:12:55.089 --> 00:12:59.600
ice-cream, and were self-service machines
invented by Satan?

00:12:59.600 --> 00:13:05.320
Organic farming uses pesticides all the time,
they are just not allowed to use any that

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:10.920
are made synthetically and they don’t use
any GMOs. There is no specific limit on the

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:17.870
volume of chemical used either so you can
end up spraying a crop 7-8 times, whereas

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:23.920
a GMO crop might only need two sprays. The
organic food market is now huge business and

00:13:23.920 --> 00:13:29.810
one of the world’s leading organic supermarkets,
Whole Foods, now has a similar revenue to

00:13:29.810 --> 00:13:38.300
Monsanto, $12.9 billion to Monsanto’s $15.9.
So don’t expect this fight to end anytime

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:42.899
soon.
The battleground of the food industry is in

00:13:42.899 --> 00:13:47.339
the supermarket. Companies scrap for prize
positions on the shelves and fight it out

00:13:47.339 --> 00:13:51.839
over prices. It’s a shame that no one has
accidentally killed off Brussels sprouts yet.

00:13:51.839 --> 00:13:56.790
No one likes you, sprouts.
Almost nothing in a supermarket is random,

00:13:56.790 --> 00:14:02.389
the entire layout and position of each product
and promotion has been carefully calculated

00:14:02.389 --> 00:14:08.630
by some very clever algorithms. The main purpose
of loyalty cards is not to make you keep coming

00:14:08.630 --> 00:14:13.509
back to the supermarket, it’s so they can
get some basic demographical information about

00:14:13.509 --> 00:14:19.290
you and then everything you buy is connected
to this data. Now, they can build profiles

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:24.589
of what people of different ages, backgrounds,
incomes etc. are buying. This then determines

00:14:24.589 --> 00:14:30.720
which products they promote and how they layout
the aisles. It’s game over as soon as you

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:35.040
walk in the door. Do you ever look down at
the mountain you’ve just built on the checkout

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:40.699
and think “All I wanted was some milk.”
But this need for supermarkets to force consumers

00:14:40.699 --> 00:14:46.269
to buy endless crap they don’t need leads
to one major problem; waste.

00:14:46.269 --> 00:14:51.190
The oranges need to be completely round, the
bananas need just the right curve, and the

00:14:51.190 --> 00:14:56.329
sprouts need that perfect flavour of disgusting.
And then everything must be labelled with

00:14:56.329 --> 00:15:00.329
various expiry dates and best before dates
and display until dates, some of which are

00:15:00.329 --> 00:15:04.930
pretty meaningless, it’s just there to act
as legal protection for the supermarket so

00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:10.920
they are always selling an optimum product.
Thanks to these factors, the world wastes

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:20.290
one third of all the food we produce. In fact,
rich countries waste about 222 million tonnes

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:27.870
every year, which is almost as much as is
produced in the whole of sub-Saharan Africa.

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:33.970
In the UK, households throw out about 6.7
million tonnes a year, despite the fact that

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:40.920
over 60% of it could have been eaten if we
had managed it better. To give you an example,

00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:47.360
the British chuck out over a million pots
of yogurt every day, unopened. But that date

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:51.839
on the yogurt doesn’t mean it’s bad, it’s
just the only date the company will guarantee

00:15:51.839 --> 00:15:57.630
the quality up to. If you’re worried about
it being full of bacteria, you don’t really

00:15:57.630 --> 00:16:01.990
understand what yogurt is.
Some countries are taking steps to limit this

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:07.230
now though and this year France passed a law
that prevents supermarkets over 400 square

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:15.190
metres in size from throwing away unsold food.
Now charities and food banks get the food

00:16:15.190 --> 00:16:20.110
for free to distribute to poor and homeless
citizens. This puts an end to the unpleasant

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:25.480
practice of deliberately spoiling food, with
bleach or other chemicals, to prevent people

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:30.949
from taking it out of the dumpsters.
Another crazy thing about the supermarkets

00:16:30.949 --> 00:16:35.149
is that when you walk in and you look at those
never ending lines of cereals and ice creams

00:16:35.149 --> 00:16:39.199
and shower gel and soda, it feels like you
have an almost infinite choice.

00:16:39.199 --> 00:16:45.100
But it’s all an illusion, especially in
the US. For Americans, almost everything in

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:50.370
the supermarket comes from just 20 companies,
and that includes organic.

00:16:50.370 --> 00:16:56.209
If it’s cereal, it’s probably Kellogg’s
or Nestle. If it’s ice cream it’s probably

00:16:56.209 --> 00:17:00.810
Unilever. If it cleans your house or your
head it’s probably Proctor &amp; Gamble, try

00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:05.220
not to get those mixed up though because no
one want Pantene on their carpets. Even meat

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:12.190
production is basically 4 companies, like
JBS who control a quarter of US cattle. In

00:17:12.190 --> 00:17:17.600
almost every area of food production, there
are just a handful of companies with a monopoly

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:21.930
over the market.
The Supermarkets themselves are also enormous.

00:17:21.930 --> 00:17:26.770
Walmart for example is the world’s largest
corporation, employing over 2 million people

00:17:26.770 --> 00:17:32.100
and taking almost $500 billion in revenue
every year, that’s more than all of South

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:38.390
Africa. Next up you have the British Tesco,
the membership only Costco and the French

00:17:38.390 --> 00:17:42.920
Carrefour. Each of these is about a quarter
the size of Walmart.

00:17:42.920 --> 00:17:47.570
It’s not all doom and gloom though. The
rise of globalisation has given many of us

00:17:47.570 --> 00:17:52.540
access to an amazing array of produce and
dishes that would have been unimaginable just

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:57.520
a couple of generations ago. Your grandparents
would have been shocked to just find strawberries

00:17:57.520 --> 00:18:02.630
in December, and here you are dipping sushi
into mango chutney, because you ran out of

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:14.700
gluten-free pumpkin spiced bagels. That is
not a good dinner by the way, what would your

00:18:14.700 --> 00:18:28.640
mother say?

